From ef4d255ae6436fab9d772e005ce95e5ef99f77c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:30:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: run the audio device at 11025Hz, not 22050/44100

Prompted by dosskutsu's own source-verified answer to the question
"how does your engine avoid the per-frame audio-pump cost dossage is
hitting on the real 486DX2-50" -- their raw-PCM path deliberately runs
the SB16 device at 11025Hz specifically because each pumped chunk then
covers ~4x more real-time audio than at 44100Hz (needing ~4x fewer
pump calls to keep pace with a frame interval), combined with avoiding
SDL core's "floor every physical device to 44100Hz" policy, which
otherwise forces a 4x logical->physical resample on every pumped
chunk regardless of what the app requests.

Two changes: (1) sampleRate dropped from 22050 to 11025 (also halves
SONG.WAV, ~5.7MB instead of ~11.4MB -- re-rendered via `make
render-music`); (2) SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate(sampleRate) called
before SDL_OpenAudioDeviceStream, using the shared backend's own
documented setter for exactly this purpose (SDL/0090) -- without it,
audioFormat.freq alone is just a request SDL's core is explicitly
licensed to override, and does (to 44100).

Verified under DOSBox-X: SDLDBG.LOG confirms "device-rate setter
default: 44100 -> 11025 Hz ... resampler collapses to channel-dup",
audio still converges to fully non-silent per SILENCE_HISTO. Real-
hardware re-verification of both fps and audio quality on the
486DX2-50 is the next step -- this is proposed as addressing the
actual mechanism dosskutsu identified, not another blind parameter
tweak.
---
 gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp |  33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp b/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
index 0ecf001..dd39cbd 100644
--- a/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
@@ -9,11 +9,27 @@
 
 #include <SDL3/SDL.h>
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+#include <SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_devicerate.h>
+#endif
+
 #include <math.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 
-int sampleRate = 22050;
-//int sampleRate = 11025;
+// DOS-PORT: 11025Hz, not 22050 (or Passage's original 44100 default).
+// Real-hardware measurement on the port's minimum-target CPU (486DX2-50)
+// found the SB PCM device-rate mismatch a major cost: SDL floors the
+// physical device to 44100Hz by default regardless of what's requested,
+// forcing a 4x logical->physical resample on every audio-ring pump and
+// proportionally more real-time-audio-per-pump-call needed at a higher
+// content rate. doskutsu's own production config (confirmed via their
+// source, not guessed) deliberately runs its SB16 device at 11025Hz
+// specifically because each pumped chunk then covers ~4x more real-time
+// audio than at 44100Hz, needing ~4x fewer pump calls to keep pace with
+// a frame interval -- combined with SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate()
+// below (forcing the physical device to actually open at this rate
+// instead of the 44100 floor), this eliminates the resampler entirely.
+int sampleRate = 11025;
 
 
 
@@ -684,11 +700,22 @@ void startMusic( const char *inTGAFileName ) {
 
     SDL_AudioSpec audioFormat;
 
-    /* Set 16-bit stereo audio at 22Khz */
+    /* Set 16-bit stereo audio at sampleRate (11025Hz -- see the comment
+       above the global) */
     audioFormat.freq = sampleRate;
     audioFormat.format = SDL_AUDIO_S16;
     audioFormat.channels = 2;
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // DOS-PORT: request the PHYSICAL device open at sampleRate too, not
+    // just the logical stream -- without this, SDL3's own core floors
+    // the real device to 44100Hz regardless of what audioFormat.freq
+    // above asks for, forcing a resample on every pumped chunk. Must be
+    // called before SDL_OpenAudioDeviceStream (cached, consumed once at
+    // OpenDevice).
+    SDL_DOSAudioSetDeviceFrameRate( sampleRate );
+#endif
+
     /* DOS-PORT: SDL3 opens a device+callback pair via
        SDL_OpenAudioDeviceStream (SDL1.2's samples/callback/userdata
        fields moved out of SDL_AudioSpec and into this call's own
-- 
2.47.3

